# Apollo 11: the landing
subtitle: "Two computer alarms, a boulder field and a fuel count in seconds: how Eagle still landed on the Moon in July 1969."
thumb: "band \"25 seconds to spare\" picture \"File:Apollo 11 AS11-40-5961HR.jpg\""

## Apollo 11: the landing
vars: {"left":94}
style: movie
sources:
    - id: alsj
      title: The First Lunar Landing (Apollo 11 Lunar Surface Journal)
      authors: Eric M. Jones, Ken Glover, Paul Fjeld et al.
      year: 1995
      finding: 'Corrected transcript with mission times: 1202 at 33,500 ft, 1201 at 3,000 ft, manual at about 550 ft, low-fuel light at 160 ft, 60/30-second calls, contact light at 20:17:40 GMT; slosh lit the quantity light early'
      url: https://apollojournals.org/alsj/a11/a11.landing.html
    - id: report
      title: Apollo 11 Mission Report (MSC-00171)
      authors: NASA Manned Spacecraft Center
      year: 1969
      finding: About 45 seconds of usable fuel at touchdown, some 20 of them reserved for an abort; landed about 4 miles long
    - id: kranz
      title: Failure Is Not an Option
      authors: Gene Kranz
      year: 2000
      finding: The last landing rehearsal before the flight was aborted needlessly over a computer alarm; the controllers then studied every alarm code

